There are now two scans for "will Go RE2 take this pattern", and they do not agree in coverage:

- lib/File/SOPS.pm `_re2_incompatible_construct` (karr #53), for a .sops.yaml
  creation rule path_regex. Text-match only, deliberately conservative:
  lookarounds and \1..\9, nothing else. It is correct for what it covers
  (verified: (?<=, (?<!, (?=, (?!, \1 all named; (?<name> not a false
  positive).
- lib/File/SOPS/Metadata.pm `_re2_divergent_construct` (karr #161,
  docs/adr/0048), for unencrypted_regex/encrypted_regex. Escape-aware,
  character-class aware, whitelist-shaped, and its verdicts were read off RE2
  itself through the path_regex oracle: the escapes RE2 rejects, possessive
  quantifiers, atomic groups, (?#), (?|), (?R)/(?1)/(?&), the (?name) quote
  spelling, (?^), every flag letter RE2 has no rule for, and the two
  constructs both dialects take and read DIFFERENTLY (\v, \Q/\E).

So a path_regex using an atomic group, a possessive quantifier, \Z, \K or (?x)
is still taken here and silently means something else at sops. They should be
one mechanism, and the Metadata one is the measured one.

Note the two croaks differ in more than wording and BOTH are right for their
own path: for a path_regex sops DOES report the compile error ("error parsing
regexp", exit 1, measured), and for an encryption rule it discards it and
matches nothing (exit 0, measured). The shared piece is the SCAN, not the
message.

Not done with karr #161 because SOPS.pm was outside that change file boundary
(the INI lane was working in it).
